<title>UNITED NATIONS: UN approves total of six contracts for Iraq.</title>
<headline>UN approves total of six contracts for Iraq.</headline>
<dateline>UNITED NATIONS 1996-12-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>The United Nations announced on Monday that its oil experts had approved six oil contracts to date with Iraq and none were pending review under the recently approved oil-for-food deal.</p>
<p>On Friday, the oil experts or overseers, had approved five contracts, which meant a sixth came in over the weekend, a spokesman said. &quot;They have approved six oil contracts with none pending,&quot; said the spokesman, without giving further details.</p>
<p>The oil-for-food deal, which came into effect on December 10 allows Iraq to sell $2 billion worth of oil over six months to buy humanitarian supplies for its people.  </p>
<p>Turkish state oil company TUPRAS received permission to buy 75,000 barrels per day (bpd) of Iraqi oil on Tuesday.</p>
<p>An additional four contracts were okayed Thursday, including contracts believed to be from U.S.-firm Coastal Corp and France's Elf Aquitaine.</p>
</text>
